-- title:
--   Monotonicity of double cones in the aperture
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$, and let $\vartheta, \vartheta' \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfy
--   $$0 \le \vartheta, \qquad \vartheta \le \vartheta', \qquad \vartheta' \le \pi .$$
--   Writing $V(v, \vartheta)$ for the double cone with axis $v$ and aperture $\vartheta$, one has
--   $$V(v, \vartheta) \;\subseteq\; V(v, \vartheta').$$
--
--   Widening the aperture of a double cone about a fixed axis enlarges it. This is the monotonicity used whenever an estimate proved for a narrow cone must be applied to a wider one, or when a cone of one aperture is replaced by a reference cone of another; it is also what allows the reduction of a configuration to a finite family of reference cones of a smaller common aperture.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Both endpoint hypotheses are needed as stated: the aperture range is confined to $[0, \pi]$, since beyond $\pi$ the angle condition defining the cone ceases to be monotone in the parameter. No hypothesis normalizes $v$; the axis is an arbitrary vector, and the statement holds in an arbitrary real inner product space rather than only in $\mathbb{R}^d$.
